00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:08.720
Polygon is a network built on top of Ethereum.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:14.080
It allows making more transactions with&nbsp;
cheaper fees than on Ethereum itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:23.360
The main token of the Polygon network is called&nbsp;
MATIC and, interestingly, apart from being the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:31.520
main token, it's also an MRC20 contract deployed&nbsp;
on the network. It allows transferring MATIC funds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:40.240
without paying a fee. The network also allows&nbsp;
you to purchase an NFT. And let's imagine it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:48.160
2030 and BBRE Premium is sold as an NFT in the&nbsp;
Polygon network. You want to purchase it. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:54.880
have some cryptocurrency but in USDT coin which&nbsp;
is not on the Polygon network but is mapped to it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:05.840
Polygon allows us to swap your tokens for my&nbsp;
NFT. Of course, the naive way to do this is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:12.880
I could tell you to send me tokens first and then&nbsp;
you would hope that I would send you the NFT back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:20.800
And I'm honest, you can trust me but we discovered&nbsp;
smart contracts for a reason. It's to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:28.240
such transactions without having to trust the&nbsp;
other person. And the way we could do this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:35.840
in Polygon network is as follows. First, we have&nbsp;
a Marketplace contract that's between me and you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:45.520
This contract will first generate an order of&nbsp;
you swapping your tokens for my NFT. It will also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:53.200
assign an order ID. Then, the marketplace&nbsp;
will ask you to generate a signature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:03:00.880
that you indeed want to swap your 500&nbsp;
USDT tokens for the BBRE Premium NFT.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:09.520
The signature will contain the address of the&nbsp;
marketplace contract, the order id from step 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:17.840
the expiration and, most importantly, how many&nbsp;
of what tokens you want to swap for what NFT.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:25.600
And you sign all that with your private key.&nbsp;
And the marketplace will ask me to do the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:34.000
thing but instead I will swap my NFT for your&nbsp;
tokens. With these two signatures, the contract&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:42.800
will now carry out both transfers and make sure&nbsp;
that either both or none go through. And for this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:49.920
the transferWithSig function is used. And pay&nbsp;
attention because this is the most important one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:55.440
in the context of this bug. It takes&nbsp;
these parameters: the signature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:04.240
the amount to transfer, the data parameter&nbsp;
is only a hash that contains the order id,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:09.600
amount and the token. It's not created&nbsp;
with a private key or anything like this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:15.680
It only prevents using this transferWithSig&nbsp;
function multiple times for the same order.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:23.680
Then, there is the expiration in the form of a&nbsp;
block number and finally, the address to which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:30.960
to send the funds. The function makes checks if&nbsp;
the amount is greater than zero, if the order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:39.040
hasn't expired and if the same transfer isn't&nbsp;
being replayed. But notice it doesn't take the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:46.880
address from which to carry out the transfer.&nbsp;
And this is a crucial information. So before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:53.440
it finally calls the transferFrom function,&nbsp;
it has to recover the address of the sender.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.960 --> 00:05:02.080
ecrecover is the function that takes the signature&nbsp;
and data of the order to retrieve the address&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:10.560
of the wallet used to create the signature.&nbsp;
Actually, it's not ecrecover but ecrecovery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:16.960
function that is used. It's only a wrapper&nbsp;
around ecrecover to make it more user-friendly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:24.880
And one of the first things it does is checking&nbsp;
if the signature is invalid. But if it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:33.040
instead of throwing an error, the function&nbsp;
returns a 0x0 address. Now, in itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:40.480
it wouldn't be a problem. Until we take a look&nbsp;
at the _transfer function. There is only a check&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:46.560
if the transfer isn't done to the same contract&nbsp;
which is absolutely not a problem for us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:54.960
In the second line, it transfers funds from&nbsp;
the contract to the recipient. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.960 --> 00:06:01.120
_transfer and transferFrom functions are only&nbsp;
created to be used internally and you can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:08.800
just call them with whatever parameters. But&nbsp;
if you could force the ecrecovery function to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:15.600
return a 0x0 address, you could transfer all the&nbsp;
funds from the contract to an arbitrary address.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:23.120
How to do it? The first line of that function&nbsp;
checks if the signature is 65 characters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:33.280
So if you submit anything other than 65 characters&nbsp;
long, you are good to go. While all that swapping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:40.240
marketplaces may have sound complicated to&nbsp;
you, the proof of concept is ridiculously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:46.640
simple. And to be honest, I don't even know if&nbsp;
the attacker really went through all these steps.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:54.160
Maybe, he just noticed this function and&nbsp;
exploited it straight away. But personally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.160 --> 00:07:01.680
I wanted to know how this transferWithSig function&nbsp;
is used in the real life. That's probably why it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:07.840
took me twice more time than usual to create notes&nbsp;
for this video but I hope you appreciate that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:14.720
So transferWithSig is an external&nbsp;
function. You can call it without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:21.120
making any marketplace transactions. Here is&nbsp;
the repository which you can clone. It creates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:27.280
the fork of the network before the patch and&nbsp;
gives you the script to reproduce the attack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:34.640
Forking is also a way to test your smart contract&nbsp;
bugs before submitting vulnerability reports.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:42.880
And this is how to call the transferWithSig&nbsp;
function. As the first argument, the signature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:50.240
use any buffer that's not 65 characters. As the&nbsp;
second, choose the amount you want to transfer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:57.600
Or, in other words, how much money you would like&nbsp;
to make. At the moment of the fix, December 5th,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:03.920
the contract had over 9 billion MATIC&nbsp;
tokens. You could steal all of them.

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:08.800
The third argument is the hash of the data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:16.400
but actually, the function only checks if the hash&nbsp;
wasn't used previously so you can use the hash of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:23.840
almost anything. Expiration also doesn't matter&nbsp;
as long as it's a block number from the future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:30.320
And finally, the recipient address which&nbsp;
means where you want to transfer the money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:37.760
And with this single function call you could&nbsp;
steal all the money that were in the contract.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:46.960
At the moment of the fix it was worth around,&nbsp;
$21 billion. And actually, before the fix was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:54.320
deployed, over eight 800,000 MATIC tokens&nbsp;
were stolen which was worth around $2 mln.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:03.520
The hunter who found the bug was rewarded&nbsp;
with $2.2 million of bounty which was above&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:08.400
the Polygon's maximum payout.&nbsp;
Even the hunter who duplicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:13.680
this report got the bounty of $1.2&nbsp;
million as an exception to the rule.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:22.160
And as for the fix, the transferWithSig&nbsp;
function was removed. Lately I've been learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:28.400
smart contract security myself. So far, I only&nbsp;
document my learning journey but with time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:34.560
I will also start creating more content. If&nbsp;
